WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.517
The checks and balances of the whole program.

2
00:00:02.797 --> 00:00:05.443
Step back from this elective to the whole program.

3
00:00:05.723 --> 00:00:14.875
Every studio taught a version of the same idea: model output is a claim, and claims need checks whose failures are independent of the thing being checked.

4
00:00:15.155 --> 00:00:24.947
Studio 1 validated structured output in code, required evidence quotes and verified them as exact substrings, and routed doubtful items to people.

5
00:00:25.227 --> 00:00:26.891
A schema is not a fact-check.

6
00:00:27.171 --> 00:00:37.880
Studio 2 measured retrieval separately from generation and required citations that point to retrieved text, because a fluent answer can be faithful to the wrong passage.

7
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:49.701
Studio 3 built evals with held-out splits, intervals, and paired comparisons, and calibrated LLM judges against human labels, because a judge is another model output.

8
00:00:49.981 --> 00:01:00.157
Studio 4 gated agent actions with permissions, sandboxes, and human approval for anything irreversible, and treated tool results as untrusted input.

9
00:01:00.437 --> 00:01:07.712
Studio 5 checked fine-tuning data for contamination and compared against a frontier baseline on held-out data.

10
00:01:07.992 --> 00:01:15.245
Studio 6 made systems observable and planned incident response, because checks that nobody watches are not checks.

11
00:01:15.525 --> 00:01:27.664
This elective applied the same logic to claims about AI itself: primary sources, dated; measured versus extrapolated; base rates; independent replication; your own small eval.

12
00:01:27.944 --> 00:01:30.995
The unifying principle is independence of checks.

13
00:01:31.275 --> 00:01:46.677
A model grading its own work, a vendor reporting its own benchmark, a lab grading its own safety framework, an analysis agent summarizing transcripts from its own model family: each is a check whose failures are correlated with the thing it checks.

14
00:01:46.957 --> 00:01:52.077
METR’s investigators were explicit about this when they relied on GPT-5.

15
00:01:52.357 --> 00:01:56.432
6 Sol to analyze agents that included GPT-5.

16
00:01:56.712 --> 00:01:57.629
6 Sol.

17
00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:09.045
The remedy is always the same: add a check that fails differently (deterministic code, a person, a different model family, an independent organization), sized to the stakes.
